      "respelling": "DAIR, DEE, DAHS",
      "ipa": "deːɐ̯, diː, das",
      "notes": "Das has a short a."
    },
    "meaning": "the",
    "shortDefinition": "German's three words for 'the', one per gender, plus changes by case."
  },
  "intro": {
    "title": "Three ways to say 'the'",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Every German noun is masculine (der), feminine (die) or neuter (das), and the article changes with the noun's job in the sentence. That's why every noun lesson here starts with its article.",
      "There are some reliable clues: -ung, -heit, -keit, -ei are always die; -chen and -lein are always das; days, months and seasons are der."
    ]
  },
  "tables": [
    {
      "title": "The article table",
      "columns": [
        "masculine",
        "feminine",
        "neuter",
        "plural"
      ],
      "rows": [
        {
          "label": "Nominative",
          "hint": "subject",
          "cells": [
            "d|er",
            "d|ie",
            "d|as",
            "d|ie"
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Accusative",
          "hint": "direct object",
          "cells": [
            "d|en",
            "d|ie",
            "d|as",
            "d|ie"
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Dative",
          "hint": "after mit, bei, von, zu…",
          "cells": [
            "d|em",
            "d|er",
            "d|em",
            "d|en …n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "Only masculine changes from nominative to accusative: der → den.",
        "Plural is always die (nominative and accusative), whatever the gender.",
        "Learn every noun with its article: der Tisch, not just Tisch."
      ]
    }
  ],
